  "id": "COMPOUND:29161 - PROTEIN:6231",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence strongly suggests a mechanistic link between the compound in question and the histone-lysine N-methyltransferase, H3 lysine-9 specific 5. This compound is identified as a target of multiple molecules, including N-Methyl-2-((4aS,4bR,9bS,10R,10aR)-2,4a,10,10a-tetrahydro-1,1,3,13-tetramethyl-1H,5H-4b,9b-(iminoethano)indeno(1,2-b)indol-10-yl)-1H-indole-3-ethanamine, Kuhlmannin, and 2-Carbomethoxy-9,10-anthraquinone. These molecules are all known to interact with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which is a key component of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2) and is involved in histone methylation, particularly at lysine 9 of histone H3. The compound in question, therefore, is likely to act through a similar mechanism, inhibiting the PRC2 complex and thereby affecting histone methylation. The consistent targeting of this compound to the H3 lysine-9 specific methyltransferase suggests a potential role in modulating gene expression by altering the methylation status of histone H3. The evidence supports a direct interaction between the compound and the target enzyme, with the compound acting as a modulator of the PRC2 complex.",
